--- !u!114 &5224622933628895591
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 5255210195740240931}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: dfb91e39d5a15b443bb75e4ad9956136, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::InfoManager
  infoTitleText: {fileID: 3777511936958018058}
  infoContentText: {fileID: 398784788310426967}
  previousInfoButton: {fileID: 1171463609586340887}
  nextInfoButton: {fileID: 5987718771937803851}
  infoBackground: {fileID: 1691492698957085339}
  chapters:
  - title: De humani corporis fabrica
    content: Vesalius was a pioneering anatomist who transformed the study of the
      human body through direct observation and careful dissection. Challenging centuries
      of accepted wisdom, he documented the structure and function of every system
      with extraordinary detail. His work revealed the complexity and interconnectedness
      of bones, muscles, organs, vessels, and nerves, showing the body not as a collection
      of separate parts but as an integrated, living whole.
    backgroundImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: caf3044da6a939448ace1313f4cdf6c5, type: 3}
  - title: I. The Bones
    content: Vesalius began with the skeletal system, emphasizing careful observation
      over inherited assumptions. He corrected misconceptions about the number and
      structure of bones, showing how they form the framework that supports and shapes
      the human body.
    backgroundImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: c3474d51848ad4047b0703161e5391d8, type: 3}
  - title: II. The Muscles
    content: In his second book, Vesalius explored muscles, describing their arrangement,
      attachment points, and function. He highlighted the interplay between bones
      and muscles, explaining how coordinated movements are produced.
    backgroundImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 48a79eafb99bec248a1ab19ca39673f9, type: 3}
  - title: III. The Veins and Arteries
    content: Vesalius mapped the vascular system with unprecedented detail. He described
      the paths of veins and arteries, noting the differences in structure and function,
      which challenged long-standing beliefs inherited from Galen.
    backgroundImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: d8f4e78290ec9c84faab619b21ced15e, type: 3}
  - title: IV. The Nervous System
    content: Focusing on the brain, spinal cord, and nerves, Vesalius examined how
      signals travel to control sensation and movement. His careful dissections revealed
      the complexity of the nervous network and its central role in the body's functions.
    backgroundImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 9bd6c9d9de7d60d48a5743849e92b3ad, type: 3}
  - title: V. The Organs of Digestion and Reproduction
    content: Vesalius analyzed the stomach, intestines, liver, and reproductive organs,
      emphasizing their structure and interrelation. He corrected errors in previous
      texts and linked form to function, showing the organs as integrated systems.
    backgroundImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 680d31d63ed4e674fbf4bfba0369df4e, type: 3}
  - title: VI. The Heart and Lungs
    content: Here, Vesalius detailed the structure of the heart, lungs, and thoracic
      cavity. He explained the mechanical aspects of circulation and respiration,
      paving the way for later discoveries about blood flow and pulmonary function.
    backgroundImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 557655a37cdf08f4a8e3e689cfffe128, type: 3}
  - title: VII. The Brain and Senses
    content: In his final book, Vesalius returned to the head, examining the brain,
      eyes, ears, and other sense organs. He described how perception and cognition
      arise from complex anatomical structures, blending physiology with observation.
    backgroundImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: b27b995223fd38942b4f6349703db7df, type: 3}
